- Green roofs, which are rooftops covered with vegetation, have been installed on over 10,000 buildings in Germany since the 1970s.
- A 2019 study in Berlin found that green roofs reduced rooftop surface temperatures by an average of 17 degrees Celsius compared to conventional roofs during summer.
- In 2009, Toronto became the first North American city to require green roofs on new commercial buildings.
- Maintenance costs for green roofs run approximately 30% higher than those for traditional roofs over a 40-year lifespan.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Although green roofs cut summer rooftop temperatures by 17 degrees Celsius on average, they cost roughly 30% more to maintain than conventional roofs over a 40-year span."},{"key":"B","text":"Germany has installed green roofs on more than 10,000 buildings since the 1970s, and in 2009 Toronto became the first North American city to require them on all newly constructed commercial properties."},{"key":"C","text":"A 2019 Berlin study determined that green roofs reduce summer rooftop surface temperatures by an average of 17 degrees Celsius when measured against the temperatures recorded on conventional roofing surfaces."},{"key":"D","text":"Because Toronto mandated green roofs on new commercial buildings beginning in 2009, building owners there have faced maintenance costs approximately 30% higher than those associated with conventional roofing over a comparable lifespan."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The stated goal is to contrast environmental benefits with financial costs. The choice beginning with "Although green roofs cut summer rooftop temperatures" achieves this by juxtaposing the 17-degree cooling benefit against the 30% higher maintenance cost, using the concessive structure "Although..." to highlight the tension. The choice about Germany and Toronto summarizes adoption history without addressing either benefits or costs. The choice focused on the Berlin study presents only the temperature reduction data without any mention of cost. The choice linking Toronto''s mandate to higher costs fabricates a causal connection not stated in the notes and omits the environmental benefit entirely, so it fails to present the contrast the goal requires.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


Ecologist Dmitri Volkov has argued that the reintroduction of gray wolves to Yellowstone in 1995 triggered a trophic cascade that fundamentally restructured the park''s riparian ecosystems. By suppressing elk populations, wolves allowed willow and aspen saplings to recover along stream banks, which in turn stabilized soils and attracted beaver colonies. Volkov describes this sequence as evidence that apex predators exert decisive top-down control over ecosystem architecture.

Text 2
Ecologist Sonia Hartwell acknowledges that elk browsing pressure on riparian vegetation decreased after wolf reintroduction but attributes the vegetation recovery primarily to a concurrent period of above-average precipitation between 1997 and 2004. Hartwell''s hydrological models indicate that moisture availability, not reduced herbivory, was the principal driver of sapling growth during this period. She cautions that attributing complex ecological outcomes to a single species oversimplifies the interplay of climatic, hydrological, and biological factors.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Both agree that wolf reintroduction caused vegetation recovery but disagree about whether beavers played a secondary role."},{"key":"B","text":"Hartwell accepts the outcome Volkov describes but attributes it to a different primary cause, arguing that climatic conditions rather than predator-driven herbivory reduction best explain the observed vegetation changes."},{"key":"C","text":"Hartwell rejects Volkov's claim that riparian vegetation recovered after wolf reintroduction, citing hydrological data showing no measurable change in sapling density."},{"key":"D","text":"Volkov and Hartwell offer complementary analyses that together confirm apex predators and precipitation jointly drive riparian ecosystem change in equal measure."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'Both ecologists agree that riparian vegetation recovered (the outcome), but they disagree about the primary cause. Volkov attributes it to wolves suppressing elk (top-down predator control), while Hartwell attributes it primarily to above-average precipitation (a climatic factor). "Accepts the outcome but attributes it to a different primary cause" precisely captures this structure of agreement on observation but disagreement on explanation. The claim that both agree wolves caused recovery but disagree about beavers misrepresents Hartwell, who disputes the wolf-driven mechanism itself. Hartwell does not reject that vegetation recovered; she disputes why it recovered. The claim that both analyses are complementary and confirm equal joint causation contradicts Hartwell''s explicit caution against attributing outcomes to a single species and her argument that precipitation, not predation, was the principal driver.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 120, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

- Linguist Keren Macaulay spent 14 years documenting the Ngarla language, spoken by fewer than 20 fluent speakers in Western Australia.
- Macaulay''s dictionary contains over 4,700 entries, including 800 terms related to marine ecology that have no direct English equivalents.
- Several Ngarla speakers contributed oral histories revealing seasonal patterns of fish migration not recorded in Western scientific literature.
- A 2022 Australian Research Council grant funded the digitization of Macaulay''s field recordings.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Keren Macaulay's 14-year effort to document Ngarla produced a dictionary of over 4,700 entries and led to the digitization of her field recordings through a 2022 Australian Research Council grant."},{"key":"B","text":"Ngarla oral histories have disclosed fish migration patterns absent from Western scientific literature, illustrating how endangered languages can harbor ecological knowledge unavailable elsewhere."},{"key":"C","text":"With the help of a 2022 Australian Research Council grant, linguist Keren Macaulay digitized her recordings of the Ngarla language, which is spoken by fewer than 20 fluent speakers in remote coastal Western Australia."},{"key":"D","text":"Because the Ngarla language includes 800 marine ecology terms without direct English translations, Western marine biologists have increasingly concluded that English is fundamentally inadequate for describing the full complexity of coastal ecosystems across the Australian continent."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The goal is to argue that language preservation yields otherwise inaccessible scientific knowledge. The choice about Ngarla oral histories disclosing fish migration patterns directly links endangered-language preservation to a concrete scientific outcome, the discovery of migration data absent from Western records, and explicitly frames this as evidence that such languages harbor unique knowledge. The choice summarizing Macaulay''s 14-year effort and the digitization grant describes the scope of the documentation project without connecting it to any scientific discovery. The choice about the Australian Research Council grant and the number of remaining speakers focuses on preservation logistics and demographic context rather than scientific yield. The choice claiming that Western biologists have concluded English is inadequate extrapolates far beyond the notes, which say nothing about biologists drawing such a sweeping conclusion.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

- JWST was launched in December 2021.
- It orbits the Sun at the second Lagrange point, approximately 1.5 million kilometers from Earth.
- Its primary mirror spans 6.5 meters, making it the largest space telescope ever deployed.
- JWST detects infrared light, allowing it to observe objects obscured by interstellar dust clouds.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Launched in 2021, the James Webb Space Telescope detects infrared light using its 6.5-meter mirror, the largest ever sent into space."},{"key":"B","text":"Because JWST detects infrared light, it can peer through the interstellar dust clouds that block the view of conventional optical telescopes orbiting closer to Earth."},{"key":"C","text":"The second Lagrange point, where JWST orbits the Sun, sits approximately 1.5 million kilometers from Earth and offers a gravitationally stable vantage point."},{"key":"D","text":"At 6.5 meters across, the primary mirror of the James Webb Space Telescope dwarfs every other space-based telescope mirror ever constructed."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The goal is to introduce JWST to an unfamiliar audience, which requires conveying what the telescope is, when it launched, and what distinguishes it. The choice beginning with "Launched in 2021" efficiently combines the launch date, the infrared detection capability, and the record-setting mirror size, giving a well-rounded introduction. The choice focusing on infrared light and dust clouds addresses only one capability without identifying the telescope broadly. The choice about the second Lagrange point emphasizes orbital mechanics, a detail too narrow to serve as an introduction. The choice about the mirror size highlights a single technical feature without contextualizing what the telescope does.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);





When repairing fractured porcelain vessels from the Ming dynasty, conservators must balance fidelity to the original form with the structural demands of long-term preservation. Using adhesives that are too rigid can <u>compromise</u> the integrity of adjacent sections, as thermal expansion causes stress fractures along repaired seams. Conversely, overly flexible bonding agents may allow subtle shifts that gradually distort the vessel''s profile.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"negotiate"},{"key":"B","text":"settle"},{"key":"C","text":"jeopardize"},{"key":"D","text":"reconcile"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'In context, "compromise" describes how rigid adhesives can damage or threaten the integrity of adjacent porcelain sections through stress fractures. "Jeopardize" means to put at risk or endanger, which matches this meaning precisely. "Negotiate" refers to reaching an agreement through discussion, which does not fit the context of material damage. "Settle" means to resolve or come to rest, which does not convey the sense of causing harm. "Reconcile" means to restore harmony between conflicting elements, which is the opposite of the damaging process described.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"vocab_context_error"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"vocab_context_error"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"vocab_context_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




Botanist Lena Okafor has argued that mycorrhizal networks — the fungal filaments connecting tree roots underground — function primarily as nutrient-sharing systems. In her 2019 study, Okafor demonstrated that phosphorus transferred through these networks moved preferentially from well-nourished trees to nutrient-deprived neighbors, suggesting a cooperative distribution mechanism.

Text 2
A 2022 study led by Raj Patel found that trees connected by mycorrhizal networks did not share nutrients equally. Instead, larger trees with more extensive root systems received a disproportionate share of the transferred phosphorus, regardless of the nutrient status of smaller trees nearby. Patel concluded that the networks amplify existing resource inequalities rather than correcting them.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By agreeing that the networks share nutrients but noting the process is not equitable across all trees."},{"key":"B","text":"By arguing that mycorrhizal networks do not transfer any nutrients between trees."},{"key":"C","text":"By suggesting that Okafor's research methods were not rigorous enough to detect phosphorus transfer."},{"key":"D","text":"By proposing that cooperation and competition are equally valid descriptions of all fungal networks."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Patel''s study confirms that nutrient transfer occurs through mycorrhizal networks (consistent with Okafor''s finding) but shows that larger trees receive disproportionate shares regardless of need, contradicting the cooperative framing. "Agreeing that networks share nutrients but noting the process is not equitable" accurately captures this partial agreement with a key qualification. Patel does not deny that transfer occurs, so the claim that networks transfer no nutrients contradicts his own findings. Patel does not question Okafor''s methodology. Patel''s conclusion specifically characterizes the networks as amplifying inequality, not as equally cooperative and competitive.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


Ornithologists tracking bar-tailed godwits across the Pacific have documented flights exceeding 11,000 kilometers without rest. The birds'' capacity to sustain such journeys depends on a metabolic transformation that begins weeks before departure: their digestive organs <u>atrophy</u> substantially, redirecting caloric resources to flight muscles and fat reserves. This pre-migratory remodeling, once considered an anomaly, is now understood as an evolved optimization strategy shared by several long-distance migrants.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"malfunction due to disease"},{"key":"B","text":"shrink through deliberate biological reallocation"},{"key":"C","text":"rupture under physical strain"},{"key":"D","text":"expand to accommodate increased nutrient processing"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'In the passage, "atrophy" describes the deliberate shrinking of digestive organs as part of a pre-migratory remodeling process that redirects resources to flight muscles and fat reserves. The passage frames this as an evolved optimization strategy, not a pathological condition. "Shrink through deliberate biological reallocation" captures both the reduction in organ size and the purposeful metabolic context. "Malfunction due to disease" implies pathology, which contradicts the passage''s characterization of the process as an evolved strategy. "Rupture under physical strain" implies violent destruction rather than gradual, adaptive reduction. "Expand to accommodate increased nutrient processing" describes the opposite of what occurs, as the organs decrease in size.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"vocab_context_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 120, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);






After the city council voted to fund a curbside composting initiative, participation rates among residents exceeded expectations. Program coordinators attributed the strong response to the campaign''s straightforward messaging, which made the benefits of composting readily <u>apparent</u> to homeowners who had never previously considered diverting food waste from landfills.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"visible"},{"key":"B","text":"obvious"},{"key":"C","text":"deceptive"},{"key":"D","text":"theoretical"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'In the passage, "apparent" describes how clearly the benefits of composting were communicated to homeowners through straightforward messaging. The word is used to mean easily understood or evident. "Obvious" captures this sense of something being easily perceived or recognized. "Visible" relates to physical sight rather than intellectual clarity. "Deceptive" means misleading, which contradicts the passage''s positive framing. "Theoretical" means existing only in concept, which contradicts the passage''s emphasis on practical, realized benefits.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"vocab_context_error"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"vocab_context_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


Researchers at the University of Bristol measured ambient sound levels in 47 European cities over a three-year period. They found that average daytime noise in commercial districts consistently exceeded 73 decibels, a threshold above which prolonged exposure has been linked to elevated cortisol levels and disrupted sleep cycles. The team noted, however, that cities with extensive tree canopy coverage along main thoroughfares recorded noise levels roughly 8 decibels lower than those without such vegetation, suggesting that urban forestry programs may offer a partial mitigation strategy.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"It presents a finding, identifies a health concern associated with that finding, and then introduces a factor that may reduce the problem."},{"key":"B","text":"It proposes a hypothesis about urban noise, tests it through controlled experiments, and confirms the original prediction."},{"key":"C","text":"It describes a policy recommendation, evaluates its effectiveness using historical data, and calls for expanded implementation."},{"key":"D","text":"It surveys multiple competing theories about noise pollution and argues that one theory is superior to the others."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The passage first presents the research finding about noise levels exceeding 73 decibels, then connects this to health consequences (elevated cortisol, disrupted sleep), and finally introduces tree canopy coverage as a factor that lowers noise levels. "Presents a finding, identifies a health concern, and introduces a mitigating factor" accurately maps this three-part structure. The passage does not describe a controlled experiment testing a hypothesis. No specific policy recommendation is evaluated. The passage presents one study''s findings rather than surveying competing theories.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


The telegram did not kill the personal letter; if anything, its brevity made the letter''s discursive intimacy more conspicuous and more valued. The telephone introduced a rival form of immediacy, yet letter-writing persisted well into the 1980s, buoyed by the tangible pleasure of paper and ink. Email, arriving in the 1990s, initially mimicked the letter''s conventions — salutations, sign-offs, paragraph breaks — as if to reassure users that the medium had changed but the social ritual had not. Only when text messaging stripped communication to its syntactic minimum did the letter lose not merely its practical function but its cultural prestige, replaced by a mode that treats elaboration as an imposition rather than a courtesy.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"The author traces a sequence of technological developments to argue that each one eroded a different aspect of letter-writing until the practice lost both utility and status."},{"key":"B","text":"The author contrasts the permanence of handwritten letters with the ephemerality of digital communication to advocate for reviving epistolary traditions."},{"key":"C","text":"The author uses a chronological survey of communication technologies to demonstrate that earlier innovations preserved letter-writing's cultural role while a later one dismantled it entirely."},{"key":"D","text":"The author presents competing scholarly interpretations of why letter-writing declined and then endorses the interpretation that emphasizes economic factors over technological ones."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'The passage moves chronologically through the telegram, telephone, email, and text messaging. For each of the first three technologies, the author argues that letter-writing survived or even benefited. Only with text messaging does the author claim the letter lost both its practical function and cultural prestige. "Uses a chronological survey to demonstrate that earlier innovations preserved letter-writing''s role while a later one dismantled it" captures this precise rhetorical arc. The reading that each technology eroded a different aspect misrepresents the passage, which explicitly states that the telegram and telephone did not diminish the letter. The passage does not advocate for reviving letter-writing. No competing scholarly interpretations or economic factors are discussed.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 120, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


Linguist Carmen Fuentes has proposed that the rapid spread of loanwords from English into Korean during the 1990s was driven primarily by South Korea''s expanding consumer electronics industry. As Korean firms marketed products globally, Fuentes argues, English technical vocabulary entered Korean not through cultural prestige but through the practical demands of international commerce and product standardization.

Text 2
Sociolinguist Yoon-Hee Park contends that commercial necessity alone cannot explain the breadth of English borrowings in modern Korean. Park''s 2021 corpus analysis showed that English loanwords adopted between 1990 and 2010 were concentrated not in technical or commercial registers but in entertainment, fashion, and social media — domains where English carries associations of modernity and cosmopolitanism that Korean equivalents lack.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Park challenges the mechanism Fuentes identifies by presenting evidence that loanword adoption was driven more by cultural associations than by commercial demands."},{"key":"B","text":"Park extends Fuentes's argument by showing that commercial vocabulary eventually spread into entertainment and fashion contexts."},{"key":"C","text":"Park supports Fuentes's thesis by confirming that technical terminology constituted the largest category of borrowed words."},{"key":"D","text":"Park reframes Fuentes's findings as applicable only to written Korean while proposing a separate theory for spoken registers."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Fuentes attributes English loanword adoption to commercial and technical demands, while Park''s corpus analysis shows the borrowings concentrated in entertainment, fashion, and social media — domains driven by cultural prestige rather than commerce. Park directly challenges the commercial-necessity explanation by offering an alternative cultural mechanism. The claim that Park extends Fuentes''s argument misrepresents the relationship; Park contradicts, not builds upon, the commercial explanation. Park''s data shows loanwords were concentrated outside technical terminology, so the claim of support through confirmation is factually wrong. Neither text distinguishes between written and spoken registers.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Deep-sea anglerfish produce light through a symbiotic relationship with bioluminescent bacteria housed in a specialized dorsal spine called the esca. The bacteria emit a blue-green glow that the anglerfish uses to lure prey in the lightless depths of the ocean. This adaptation is not unique to anglerfish; over 1,500 species of fish are known to produce or harness bioluminescence for predation, communication, or camouflage.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"It broadens the discussion from one species to a wider biological phenomenon."},{"key":"B","text":"It provides a counterexample that challenges the claim made in the first sentence."},{"key":"C","text":"It raises a question about the reliability of research on deep-sea organisms."},{"key":"D","text":"It narrows the focus to a specific subspecies of anglerfish found at extreme depths."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The passage begins by describing the anglerfish''s bioluminescent mechanism, then the third sentence explicitly states that this adaptation is shared by over 1,500 fish species, expanding the scope from a single organism to the broader phenomenon. "Broadens the discussion from one species to a wider biological phenomenon" accurately describes this structural move. The sentence does not challenge any prior claim but rather extends it, so the counterexample reading is wrong. No question about research reliability is introduced. The sentence moves outward to more species, not inward to a subspecies.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-10-18"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-10-18","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);





